    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_1eae93a20da6ec2cd02600c5f48f4a35b8d6c709146685d6cb0068f64f763e84",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Time averaging of short prime cosine products has no main term when some prime occurs with odd multiplicity; for even multiplicities the surviving coefficient is multiplicative and factors prime by prime. Positivity-preserving Taylor truncations or Bessel generating products package these admissible diagonal terms into Gaussian-scale moments. This arithmetic mechanism is parallel to, but not the same as, the random-matrix mechanism in which a diverging variance and bounded higher cumulants yield a normalized Gaussian law.",
